The genealogy of Jesus.
This is a record of the lineage of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. And Isaac was the father of Jacob. And Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
The inheritance of Jacob, of the sons of his uncle Laban. And the name of his wife's female servant was Zilpah. And the name
And Ram was the father of Amminadab, who was the father of Nachshon, who was the father of Salmon.
Salmon is the father of Boaz. Rahab is the mother of Boaz. Boaz is the father of Obed. Ruth is the mother of Obed. Obed is the father of Jesse.
And Jesus the son of David the king. And David the son of Jesse. And Jesse the son of Obed.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Abu Jhozhavsaphat. Jhozhavsaphat Abu Jozaram. Jozaram Abu Uzzijah.
Uzziah the father of Jotham, Jotham the father of Ahaz, Ahaz the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah is the father of Manasseh. Manasseh is the father of Amon. Amon is the father of Josiah.
Josiah became the father of Jehoiachin and his brothers in the days of the Babylonian captivity.
And afterward the genealogy continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. And Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Uzaz father of Sadok, Sadok father of Achim, Achim father of Eleoud.
He is the father of Elazar. Elazar is the father of Matan. Matan is the father of Yaqub.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. Mary gave birth to Jesus who is called the Christ.
So, there were fourteen generations from Abraham to David. And there were fourteen generations from David until the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon. And there were fourteen generations from that time to Christ. And Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother was Mary and Joseph had made a commitment to marry her. But before they came together, it became clear that she would give birth to a child. She became pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Joseph, was a pious man. He did not want to put her in a shameful situation before people. Therefore, he planned to quietly divorce her.
But Joseph was thinking about this, when an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said to him, "Joseph son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary your wife, for the child who is conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You shall name him Jesus, for he will save his people from their sins."
And all this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet,
Behold, the virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and they shall call his name Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name "Immanuel" means "God with us".
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not have relations with her until she gave birth to a son, and he called his name Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in the region of Judea. This happened during the time when Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east arrived in Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the one who was born king of the Jews? For we saw his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When Herod the king heard of it, he was exceeding wroth, and sent forth, and slew all the males children that were in Bethlehem, and in all the coasts thereof.
When Herod the king heard this, he was disturbed, and all Jerusalem with him. When he had gathered all the chief priests and scribes of the people, he inquired of them where the Christ was to be born.
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered Him. "For this is written by the prophet, 'And you Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, are not least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'"
"«But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come for me one who will be ruler over Israel. Whose origin is from of old, from ancient days. I will be
Then Herod secretly summoned the Magi. He discovered from them the exact time the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. He said, "Go! And diligently search for the child. And when you have found him, bring me word. Then I can also go and worship him."
And when the magi had listened to the king, they departed on their way. And the star which they had seen when it rose went before them, until it came to rest over the place where the child was.
And when they saw the star, they rejoiced with exceedingly great joy.
The magi went to the house. And there they saw the child with his mother Mary. So they fell down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. And they presented him with gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
And God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another route. The flight of Jesus' family to Egypt
And when the magi had departed, behold, the angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, saying, "Arise, and take the young child and his mother, and flee into Egypt, and be there until I bring you word. For Herod the king is about to seek the young child to destroy him."
Joseph got up. And during the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They stayed there until King Herod died. Then the words spoken by the Lord through the prophet came true, saying, "I have indeed called my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11: 1)
Herod realized that the Magi had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. That all the boy children who were under the age of two were to be killed. This was in agreement with the time that the Magi had seen the star.
In this way, the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said:
A voice is heard in Ramah, lamentation and great weeping. Rachel weeps for her children, refusing to be comforted because they are gone." (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth
And after Herod's death, when Joseph was in Egypt, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream, saying:
The angel said, "Arise! Take the child and his mother. Go into the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
Then Joseph arose, and took the Child and His mother Mary, and returned with them to the land of Israel.
But when he heard that Archelaus was reigning in Judea in place of his father Herod, he became afraid to go there. And after being warned in a dream, he departed to the regions of Galilee.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. So, what the prophets said about Jesus came true. They said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. For he said, "A voice of one crying in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel's hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
And Jerusalem and all Judea and all the regions around the Jordan went out to him.
And when they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?"
Produce fruit that shows you have turned away from your sins.
Do not think and say within yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe is already laid at the roots of the trees. Every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down and thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent of your sins. But after me, one will come who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to untie the thong of his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and with fire.
And he took him by the hand and led him out of the city, to a hill on which there was a large cave, and in front of the cave a large stone; and the cave was used as a tomb.
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. He said to Jesus, "I must be baptized by you. And why do you come to me?"
Jesus said, "Let it be so now. It is right for us to do this. It fulfills God's holy plan." Then John agreed.
And when Jesus was baptized, he went up immediately from the water. And behold, the heavens were opened to him, and he saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove upon him.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
The Temptation of Jesus
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting and not eating, Jesus felt hungry.
The tempter came to him and said, "If you are the Son of God, command that these stones become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city and set him on the pinnacle of the temple.
"If you are the Son of God," said the Devil to Him, "cast Yourself down, for it is written: 'He shall give His angels charge concerning you, and in their hands they shall bear you up, lest at any time you dash your foot against a stone.' And the devil took Him up into the holy city, and set Him on a pinnacle of the temple, and said to Him, 'If You are the Son of God, throw Yourself down from here, for it is written: "He shall give His angels charge concerning you, and in their hands they shall bear you up, lest at any time you dash your foot against a stone."' Jesus said to him, 'It is written: "You shall not tempt the Lord your God."' And the devil left Him, and behold, angels came and ministered to Him. And behold, Jesus, full of the Holy Spirit, returned from the
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone.' " (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said, "If you prostrate to me and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus begins to preach.
John was imprisoned, and when Jesus heard about it, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the town of Capernaum. It was by the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
And in this way, what the prophet Isaiah had said was fulfilled. For he had said:
O land of Zebulun! O land of Naphtali! Galilee, where the Gentiles dwell! A land along the Mediterranean Sea! A territory east of the Jordan River!
The people walking in darkness have seen a great light. Those walking in a dark land, have seen a great light. But a light has shined upon them." (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
From that time on, Jesus began to preach, saying, "Repent of your sins!" And he said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus Chooses the First Disciples
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me." "I will make you fishers of people."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
And he left there, and saw two other brothers. They were James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. And they were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And they were preparing to spread their nets, when Jesus called to them.
When they got out of the boat and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of God's kingdom. He healed every disease and illness that afflicted the people.
His news spread throughout Syria. People would bring him all the sick, with various diseases. Some suffered from severe pain. Some were possessed by demons. Some trembled violently. Some couldn't move at all. Jesus healed them all.
A great throng followed him. People came from Galilee, from that region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from beyond the Jordan River.
Jesus grants blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on the mountainside and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
