Not far away, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, saying, "If you drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" And the demons came out of the people and entered the pigs. The entire herd of pigs then rushed down the steep bank, ran to the lake, and drowned in the water.
Those who used to feed the pigs fled. They entered the city and reported all these things. Therefore, they mustered detachments, saying, "There has risen up among us a people who corrupt the land."
Then all the people of the city went out to welcome Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus got into a boat. He went to the other side of the lake and reached his hometown.
Some men brought to Him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. Seeing that they had faith, Jesus said to the man, "Do not be afraid, son, your sins are forgiven."
Then some of the teachers of the law said to themselves, "This man speaks blasphemies! Who can forgive sins but God alone?"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. So he said, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?
Which is easier, to say, Your sins are forgiven, or to say, Rise, take up your bed and walk?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then he spoke to the man who could not walk. "Get up," he said. "Take your mat and go home."
The man got up and went home.
When the crowd saw this, they were amazed and glorified God, who gives such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
Jesus went away from there, and saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Matthew rose and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this, and they asked his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard about this. And he said, "It is not those who are well who need a physician, but those who are ill.
Go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy and not sacrifice.' (Hosea 6:6) I did not come to call those who think themselves righteous before God to follow me. I came to call sinners to follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John came. They said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees do not eat. Why then do your disciples not go without eating?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom is taken from them; then they will fast.
A new cloth cannot be patched onto old clothes, because the new patch will tear from the old clothes, and this will make the tear even worse.
"O people, do not put new wine into old wine skins. Otherwise, the skins will burst, the wine will spill, and the skins will be ruined. No, each person should put new wine into new wine skins. This way, both will be preserved." Dead girl and suffering woman.
While Jesus was saying this, a ruler came and knelt down before Jesus. He said, "My daughter has just died. But come and put your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus rose up and went with him. And so did his disciples.
And when Jesus passed along with his disciples, a crowd of people followed him, and among them was a woman who had a flow of blood for twelve years, and she had spent all her money on doctors, and she got no benefit from it at all, but rather became worse than before. But when she saw Jesus, she came out of the crowd and touched the fringe of his robe, and he turned and looked at her and said to her, Woman, your faith has made you well. And immediately her condition changed from illness to health.
"I only need to touch his clothes. Then I will be healed," I thought.
Jesus turned and saw her. He said, "Woman, great is your faith. Do not be afraid, for your faith has saved you." And in that moment, the woman was healed of her disease.
When Jesus entered the chief official's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go away, the girl is not dead, she is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowd was sent outside, Jesus entered. He took the hand of the girl, and she arose.
News about what Jesus had done spread all over that region. Jesus heals two blind men.
And as Jesus was passing by from there, two blind men followed Him. And they called out, saying, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
And when Jesus entered the house, the two blind men came to him. And he asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" And they told him, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes. And he said, "It shall be done to you as you have believed."
They were able to see again. Jesus warned them severely: "Be sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news, and talked about it in all that region.
While they were going out, another man was brought to Jesus. An evil spirit controlled him, and he could not speak.
When the unclean spirit had been driven out, the man spoke. The crowd was astonished. They said, "Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are only a few laborers
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every affliction.
When he saw the crowds, he had compassion for them, because they were like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few.
Ask then the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his vineyard.
Jesus sends the twelve disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. And he gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal all sickness and disease.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first of them is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
Then Philip and Bartholomew, Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. Then James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
And the others are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. And Judas is the one who handed Jesus over after that to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve and gave them these instructions: "Do not go to Gentiles," he said. "Do not enter any Samaritan town."
Go to the people of Israel and say to them, 'You are like a flock of sheep that have gone astray.'
As you go, begin with this teaching: "The Kingdom of the heavens is near."
Heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse the lepers, cast out demons. Freely you have received, freely give.
Do not carry gold or silver or bronze in your belts.
Do not take a suitcase for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be given what he needs.
When you enter a town or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at that person's home until you leave.
If you enter the house, greet its people.
If that house welcomes you, then bless it with your peace. But if it is not so, then do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. If they do not, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am about to tell you is true. On the day of judgment it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I am sending you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves.
Beware! People will hand you over to local courts. And they will flog you in their temples.
You will be brought before governors and kings because of me, and you will testify to them and to the non-Jewish nations.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words will be given to you to say.
It will not be you who speaks. Instead, the spirit of your father is the one who will speak through you.
Brothers will betray their brothers to be killed. Fathers will betray their sons. Sons will rise up against their fathers and be executed.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But everyone who stands firm to the end will be saved.
"When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I am about to tell you is true. You will not have finished going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes."
The student is not better than his teacher. The servant is not better than his master.
It is enough for a student to be like his teacher, and enough for a servant to be like his master. If the master of the house is called Beelzebul, what will the condition of the people of that house be?
Do not fear your enemies. For everything that is secret will be revealed, and everything that is hidden will be made known.
What I tell you in secret, proclaim on the housetops. And do not fear those who kill the body but cannot
Do not fear those who kill the body and after that have nothing more they can do. Instead, fear the one who has the power to destroy both body and soul in hell.
Aren't two sparrows sold for a farthing? And one of them falls not on the ground without your Father.
He even counts every single hair on your head!
Fear not. You are the best of many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make the son rise against his father, the daughter against her mother, and the daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.
"The enemy of a man is his own household." (Micah 7:6)
Whoever loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. And whoever loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And everyone who does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever loses their life for me will find it. Whoever loses their life for me because of who I am, will find it.
He who receives you receives me, and he who receives me receives the One who sent me.
Let's suppose that someone receives a prophet as a prophet. This person will receive the reward of a prophet. And let's suppose that someone receives a pious person as a pious person. This person will receive the reward of a pious person.
If anyone, even with a cup of cold water, gives something to a young child who is following me, then what I am about to say is absolute truth. That person must be rewarded.
Jesus and John the Baptist.
Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples. Then he went out to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard about the works of Christ, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come, or shall we look for someone else?"
Jesus said, "Go to John, and say to him what you hear and see."
The blind see. The lame walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised to life. And the poor have good news preached to them.
Blessed are those who do not insist on their faith because of me.
And as John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began speaking to the crowd about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet. This is the one about whom it is written: 'Behold, I send my messenger before your face, who will prepare your way before you.' I tell you, among those born of women there is no greater prophet than John the Baptist, but the least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he."
If it is not so, what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. The people who wear fine clothes are in the palaces of kings.
So, what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one about whom it was written in the Holy Book. It says: "'Behold, I will send My messenger, and he will prepare the way before
What I am about to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he is.
For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace. Isaiah 9:6-7
Every prophet and every dwelling place prophesied until John came.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Let him who has ears hear.
What are the people of today like? They are like children sitting in the markets, calling out to each other. They say,
We played the reed for you, but you did not dance. We sang a sad song for you, but you were not sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink, and people said, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, 'This man, he is always eating and drinking too much. He is a friend of tax collectors and "sinners." ' Those who act as wise men prove that wisdom is right." The cities that do not turn away from sin
Jesus began to speak against the cities where He had performed most of His miracles. The people there had not turned away from their sins. So He said,
Oh, what terrible things will befall you, Chorazin! Oh, what terrible things will befall you, Bethsaida! If the miracles that have been performed among you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented of their sins long ago. They would have worn sackcloth, sat in ashes.
But I tell you: it will be easier for Tyre and Sidon on the day of judgment than for you.
And what about you, O Kufranakhum? Will you be lifted up to the heavens? No! Rather, you will descend to the place of the dead. Assume that the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom. Then, he would be here today.
But I tell you this. On the day of judgment it will be easier for Sodom than for you." Comfort for all who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "I praise you, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and understanding and revealed them to children.
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
My father handed over everything to me. Only the Father knows the Son. Those who know the Father are the Son alone, and those whom the Son reveals.
Come to me, all of you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Be my disciples, and learn from me. For I am gentle and lowly in heart. And you will find rest for your souls.
"My service is easy and my burden is light."
Jesus is the Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath day, Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pick some grain stalks and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this. And they said to Jesus: "Look! It is against the law for him to do this on the Sabbath. But your disciples do it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read what David did, when he was hungry, he and his companions?"
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were to eat it.
Have you not read the Law? It describes how that every Sabbath day, the priests in the temple must do their work on that day. But they are not considered guilty.
I tell you that there are those here who are more important than the temple.
The Holy Book says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) You do not know the meaning of these words. If you knew, you would not have brought accusations against those who are innocent.
"Indeed, the Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath."
And leaving that place, Jesus entered their synagogue.
And there was a man who had a withered hand, and the Pharisees were seeking to find Jesus doing something wrong, so they asked Him saying, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbaths?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep fell into a pit on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you reach in and pull it out?"
The value of a human being is greater than the value of a sheep! And therefore, the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was restored, just as sound as the other.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew all the plans of the Pharisees. Therefore, he left that place. Many followed him, and he healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to reveal his identity.
This was to fulfill what was spoken by the prophet Isaiah, who said,
Here is my servant whom I uphold, my chosen one in whom I delight; I place my Spirit on him, and he will bring justice to the nations.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bent branch. He will not extinguish a dim flame. He will make everything alright.
"That they may see the nations inquire of Him whose name was invoked upon Jerusalem, and were told, 'This is the hand of the Lord, which is foretold by His prophets.'"
A man who was seized by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and could not speak. Jesus healed him. Then the man was able to speak and see.
And all the people were amazed at the wonderful things he was doing. They said, "Is this the son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow is casting out demons by the prince of demons."
Jesus knew their thoughts. He said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation; and every city or house divided against itself will not stand."
If the devil drives out the devil, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom endure?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul. By what power then do your people cast out demons? Whoever is not with me is against me, and whoever does not gather with me scatters. Therefore they will be your judges.
But suppose I cast out demons by the Spirit of God. Then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or think about this. How can you break into a strong man's house and simply take what the man owns? You must first tie him up. Then you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me. And he who does not gather with me scatters.
Here is what I say to you: Every sin and blasphemy against God will be forgiven, but blasphemy against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
And whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven; but whoever blasphemes against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If the tree is good, its fruit will be good. And if the tree is bad, its fruit will be bad. The shape of the tree is known by its fruit.
O nest of poisonous vipers! How can you evil people say anything good? For out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks.
The righteous man speaks of righteousness. This stems from the goodness which is stored within him. The wicked man speaks of wickedness. This stems from the wickedness which is stored within him.
But that is what I am telling you. On the day of judgment, people will be held accountable for every careless word they have spoken.
With your words, you will be condemned or acquitted." The miraculous sign of Leona.
And some of the Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered and said, "Evil and adulterous people seek for a sign, but no sign will be given to them except the sign of the prophet Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of the great fish three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will rise up in the judgment with this generation, and Nineveh will rise up against this generation and condemn it. The men of Nineveh repented of their sins at the preaching of Jonah. And now there is a greater than Jonah among them.
The Queen of the South will rise up on the day of judgment with this generation and will condemn them. She came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now, here is one greater than Solomon.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a person? It goes through dry places looking for a place to rest. But it does not find it.
Then you say, "I will go back to the house I left." And when you get there, you find the house empty. The house has been swept, cleaned, and put in order.
"Then the evil spirit goes out and takes with it seven other spirits more wicked than itself, and they enter and live there. And the condition of that man becomes worse than it was before. And so will be the fate of the wicked in this time." The brothers and sisters of Jesus.
While Jesus was still talking to the crowds, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
Someone told him: "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to speak to you."
Jesus said to him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. And he said, "Behold, my mother! Behold, my brothers!"
Whoever does the will of the one who is in the heavens, he is my brother and my sister and my mother.
The Farmer's Story
That very day, Jesus left the house and sat by the Sea of Galilee.
And a large crowd followed him. So he went up to a boat. And he sat in it. And all the people standing on the shore.
Then he told them many things using stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds.
He sowed the seeds on the ground. Some of it fell on the road. The birds came and ate it.
Some seeds fell on rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants sprouted quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Some seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and crushed the crop.
Some seeds fell on fertile ground, which produced a yield that was 100, or 60, or 30 times greater than that of the corresponding cultivated variety.
Let him who has ears hear.
And the disciples came to him and asked, "Why do you speak to the people in parables?"
He answered, "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. They have not been given to outsiders.
And whoever has that knowledge will be given more. In fact, they will have very much. And if anyone does not have that knowledge, even what little he has will be taken from him.
Here's why I use stories when I talk to people. I say: "They look, but they don't really see. They listen, but they don't really hear or understand."
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said: "You will hear but not understand. You will see but not perceive. For the hearts of this people have become dull. Their ears are hard of hearing, and they have closed their eyes, lest they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and turn and be healed."
These people's hearts are hardened. They barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, they might see with their eyes. They might hear with their ears. They might understand with their hearts. They might return to the Lord, and then he would heal them." (Isaiah 6:9,10)
But blessed are your eyes, for they see; and your ears, for they hear.
What I am about to tell you is real. Many prophets and righteous people have desired to see what you see. But they did not see it. They desired to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here's the meaning of the farmer's story.
People hear the message about the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what had been sown in their hearts. Those people are like the seed sown along the path.
And others received the seed that fell on rocky ground. These are the ones who hear the word and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots, and so they do not last long. Instead, they quickly fall away when persecution or hardship comes because of the message.
And others received the seed that fell on the path; these are the ones who hear the word, but the worries of this world and the deceitfulness of wealth choke it, and it does not bear fruit.
The translation of the provided text is as follows: But some of them accepted the seed that fell on good ground. They are those who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop 100, 60 or 30 times more than the farmer who sowed." The Story of the Reeds
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "Thus is the kingdom of heaven." "A man sowed good seed in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then went away.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's servants came to him. They said, 'Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did these weeds come from?'
He replied, 'An enemy has done this.' The servants asked him, 'Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?'
The owner replied, 'No, while you are weeding, you might uproot the wheat along with it.'
Let both grow together until harvest. At that time I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will tell them. First gather the weeds. Bind them into bundles to burn. Then gather the wheat. Bring it into my barn." "Stories of the Mustard Seed and the Yeast
Jesus told the crowd another story. He said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field.
It is the smallest seed among all the seeds you have. But when it grows, it becomes the largest plant in the garden. It turns into a tree. Birds come and rest on its branches.
He told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast that a woman took and mixed with three measures of flour until it worked all through the dough."
And Jesus spoke all these things to the crowd using parables. He did not say anything to them without speaking in a parable.
So, the words spoken by the prophet were fulfilled. He had said, "I will open my mouth in parables. I will utter things hidden since the foundation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explains the parable of the weeds
Then Jesus went away from the crowd, and entered the house. And his disciples came to him. They said, "Explain to us the parable of the tares of the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. And the harvest is the day of judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are uprooted and burned in the fire. So it will be on the day of judgment.
And the son of man will send his angels. And they will gather out of his kingdom all things that cause sin. And they will gather out also all that do iniquity.
They will cast them into Gehenna. There they will mourn and gnaw their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear. Tales of the Hidden Treasure and the Pearl
The kingdom of the heavens is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
Again, the Kingdom of Heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls.
He found a pearl of great value. So he went and sold all that he had. And he bought that pearl. The story of the net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake. It caught all kinds of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. As for the bad fish, they threw them away.
This is how it will be on the Day of Judgment. The angels will come, and they will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
The wicked will be thrown into the burning fiery furnace. There the wicked will wail and grind their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He said to them, "Every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a household who brings out from his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
Then he left from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began to teach people in their synagogue. They were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get this power to perform miracles?" they wondered.
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother's name Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?"
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, and no prophet is accepted in his own home."
There were only a few miracles performed there because of their lack of faith.
John the Baptist's head was cut off.
At that time Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and put him in prison because of Herodias, the wife of his brother Philip.
John said to Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. Herod was greatly pleased.
He vowed with oaths to give her everything she asked for.
Herodias's daughter said to her mother, "What shall I ask for?" The girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very displeased. But he thought of his promise and of his dinner guests. Therefore, he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod put John the Baptist to death in prison.
His head was brought on a large plate and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
So the disciples of John came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowd heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came to the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep compassion for them. He healed their sick.
And when the sun was about to set, his disciples came to him and said, "There is no food here, and it is time to send the crowds away so they can go to the villages and buy themselves food."
Jesus said, "They do not need to go. You give them something to eat."
And they said, "We have here only five loaves and two fish,".
He said, "Bring them here to me."
Then Jesus instructed the crowd to sit on the grass. He took five loaves of bread and two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. Then he broke the loaves and gave them to the disciples. The disciples distributed them to the crowd.
They all ate and were satisfied. Then the disciples picked up twelve baskets of leftover pieces of food.
There were about 5,000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
And immediately Jesus made the disciples get into the boat, and He sent them ahead to the other side, while He was dismissing the crowds.
And after he had sent them away, he went up by himself into a mountain to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat had gone far out to sea. The waves were crashing against it because the wind was blowing in the opposite direction.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake, and they trembled with fear. "He is a ghost!" they cried. And their screams rose in terror.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, crying, "Be courageous, I am he, do not be afraid."
Peter asked, "Lord, is it you?" "If it is you, tell me to come to you walking on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid and began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
And immediately Jesus stretched out his hand and grasped him, saying, "Your faith is very little!" And he asked him, "Why did you doubt Me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
And those in the boat worshiped him, saying, "Truly you are the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and came ashore at Gennesaret.
The men who lived there knew Jesus. So they sent a message throughout the nearby countryside. People brought all their sick to Jesus.
They knelt before him, asking him to allow the sick to simply touch the edge of his cloak. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "impure"?
And they came from Jerusalem Sadducees and Pharisees; and they spake before him, asking him, and saying,
Why do your disciples not obey what the elders teach? Your disciples do not wash their hands before they eat!
Jesus answered: "Why do you not obey God's command? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) He also said, "If anyone curses his father or his mother, he will be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their parents: "Any help you may have received from us is a gift intended for God."
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents with a gift. You are making the Word of God useless in order to follow your own teachings.
O you hypocrites! Isaiah was true when he prophesied about you, saying:
These people honor me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They know nothing but human laws." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. And He said, "Hear and understand.
"That which enters into your mouth does not defile you; but that which proceeds out of the mouth, that defiles a man."
Then the disciples came to him. They asked, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are some plants that my Father in heaven did not plant; those plants will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind person leads another blind person, they will both fall into a pit."
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Haven't you understood yet?" asked Jesus them.
Don't you see? Whatever goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
For out of the heart come evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, false testimony, slander. These are what defile a person.
Bad thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and all sexual immorality. Also theft, false witness, and slander.
Those are the things that make you 'unclean.' But eating without washing your hands does not make you 'unclean.' "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman" " إيمان امرأة من كنعان"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
And when she passed by the side of Tirya and Sidon, and when she was near Tirya and Sidon, she came to him and cried out saying, O my Lord, O daughter of David have mercy on me, for behold, a demon is oppressing my daughter, and she is being tormented with great torment.
Jesus did not say a word. Therefore, his disciples came to him. They begged him: "Send her away. She keeps crying out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. They are like lost sheep."
Then the woman knelt before him. "Oh Lord! Help me!" she said.
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
"Yes, Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' tables."
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that hour. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus left that place. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to the foot of a mountain and sat down.
And great multitudes followed him, and he healed them all.
The people marveled. Those who were not able to speak were speaking. The disabled became healthy. Those who were not able to walk were walking. Those who were blind could see. Therefore, the people glorified the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. And he said, "I have compassion for these people, because they have already been with me three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, lest they faint on the way."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here." And they said, "Where can we find enough bread to satisfy such a large crowd?"
Jesus asked, "How many loaves do you have?" They replied, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus told the crowd to sit on the ground.
Taking the seven loaves and the two fish, He gave thanks. Then, breaking them, He gave them to the disciples, and the disciples distributed them to the people.
So they all ate and were satisfied. Then, when the disciples took seven baskets full of leftovers, fragments, and pieces that were left over, they were filled with amazement at His power and at the provision He had made for these people.
There were 4000 men who ate. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowd away, he went up into the boat. He went to the region near Magadon.
Jesus was asked about a miraculous sign.
And the Pharisees and Sadducees came, testing Jesus. They asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He answered, "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, "Today will be windy. The sky is red and cloudy." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
Indeed, some people are evil and treacherous; they are looking around for a miraculous sign. But no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The students crossed to the other side of the lake. They forgot to bring bread with them.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out." "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils discussed this among themselves. They said, "He must be saying this because we didn't bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. "You have so little faith!" he said. "Why are you talking to each other about having no bread?"
Do you not yet understand? Do you not remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you picked up?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the 4000? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you collected?
How is it that you do not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
At that time, the disciples understood that Jesus was not warning them to beware of the leaven used for bread, but was warning them about what was being taught by the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Messiah.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "What do people say about the Son of Man?"
They answered, "Some say he is John the Baptist; others say he is Elijah; and still others say he is Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Then Simon Peter answered him, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus said, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven."
Here I say to you: You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church, and the gates of Hades will not overpower it.
Here I give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. For whatever is bound on earth shall be bound in heaven. And whatever is loosed on earth shall be loosed in heaven."
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus prophesies his imminent death.
And from that time, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There, he must suffer many things from the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed, and on the third day, he would rise from the dead.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "No, Lord!" Peter said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me. You do not have in mind the concerns of God, but merely human concerns."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "If anyone wants to come after me, he must deny himself and take up his cross and follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What profit is to a man if he gains the whole world and loses his own soul? Or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?
And the son of man will come in the glory of his father with his angels, and he will repay each one according to his deeds.
That which I am about to say unto you is truth. Verily, some of those who stand here shall not taste death, until they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom."
Jesus' appearance changed.
And after six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John, and brought them up onto a high mountain by themselves.
And there before them, his form changed. For his face shone like the sun. And his clothes became white like light.
And when Moses and Elijah had appeared to them by His side. And Moses and Elijah were speaking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make here for you three dwellings. One for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And as Peter was speaking, a bright cloud came over them. And a voice came from the cloud, saying, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard that, fear fell into their hearts, and they fell face down to the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them. "Rise up," he said. "Do not be afraid."
And when they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And as they were coming down, Jesus told them what to do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has been raised from the dead."
His disciples asked him, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and make everything new again."
But I tell you: Elijah has come already, and people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will also make the Son of Man suffer."
Then the disciples understood that Jesus spoke to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy possessed by a demon.
And when they came near the crowd, a man went up to Jesus. And he knelt down before him.
"O Lord," he said, "have mercy on me for the sake of my son. He is shaking violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.
" You people, you wicked and evil generation! " Jesus answered. " How much longer must I be with you? How much longer must I put up with you? Bring the boy here to me."
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. He was healed at that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, "Why could we not cast out the demon?"
He answered, "Because you have so little faith. I tell you the truth, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
.
And they gathered to Galilee. And Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man is to be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." At this, the disciples were filled with sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax.
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Does not your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does that," he answered. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "From whom do the kings of the earth collect taxes and duties? Do they collect from their own sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children ought not to pay," Jesus said unto him.
But we don't want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them for the tax for me and for you.
